    "format": "subjective",
    "subtopic": "Integration by Parts",
    "difficulty": "HARD",
    "stem": "Find \\(\\int \\frac{x \\sin^{-1} x}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}} \\, dx\\).",
    "solution": "Let the first function be \\(\\sin^{-1} x\\) and the second function be \\(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}\\). First find the integral of the second function \\(\\int \\frac{x\\, dx}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}\\): put \\(t = 1 - x^{2}\\), then \\(dt = -2x\\, dx\\), so \\(\\int \\frac{x\\, dx}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}} = -\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{dt}{\\sqrt{t}} = -\\sqrt{t} = -\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}\\). Hence \\(\\int \\frac{x \\sin^{-1} x}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}\\, dx = (\\sin^{-1} x)(-\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}) - \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}}(-\\sqrt{1-x^{2}})\\, dx = -\\sqrt{1-x^{2}}\\,\\sin^{-1} x + x + C = x - \\sqrt{1-x^{2}}\\,\\sin^{-1} x + C\\). (Alternatively, substitute \\(\\sin^{-1} x = \\theta\\) and then integrate by parts.)"
