    "subtopic": "Integration by Parts",
    "difficulty": "HARD",
    "stem": "Find \\(\\int e^{x} \\sin x \\, dx\\).",
    "solution": "Take \\(e^{x}\\) as the first function and \\(\\sin x\\) as the second function. Integrating by parts, \\(I = \\int e^{x} \\sin x \\, dx = e^{x}(-\\cos x) + \\int e^{x} \\cos x \\, dx = -e^{x}\\cos x + I_1\\) (say) ...(1). Taking \\(e^{x}\\) and \\(\\cos x\\) as the first and second functions in \\(I_1\\), we get \\(I_1 = e^{x} \\sin x - \\int e^{x} \\sin x \\, dx\\). Substituting \\(I_1\\) in (1): \\(I = -e^{x}\\cos x + e^{x}\\sin x - I\\), or \\(2I = e^{x}(\\sin x - \\cos x)\\). Hence \\(I = \\int e^{x} \\sin x \\, dx = \\frac{e^{x}}{2}(\\sin x - \\cos x) + C\\)."
  },
